1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,940
Your board wakes, reads two sensors, sends one line through the air, and sleeps.

2
00:00:06,940 --> 00:00:10,860
Check the drivers and paste both files. We'll test with the terminal first.

3
00:00:11,860 --> 00:00:20,879
Line 85 helps build text. Lines 91 to 93 handle sleep. Lines 94 to 96: tilt, voltage

4
00:00:20,879 --> 00:00:24,100
and the Bluetooth serial port.

5
00:00:25,100 --> 00:00:28,300
Switch on lines 120 to 124.

6
00:00:28,800 --> 00:00:35,920
Five seconds of sleep, wake source zero, serial port one, and 9600 speed.

7
00:00:36,920 --> 00:00:42,840
Switch on lines 155 to 188. These are the sleep pieces from demo one.

8
00:00:43,340 --> 00:00:47,900
Lines 155 to 163 tidy up after waking.

9
00:00:48,400 --> 00:00:52,400
Lines 165 to 178 set the alarm.

10
00:00:52,900 --> 00:00:58,020
Lines 180 to 188 start it and sleep.

11
00:00:59,020 --> 00:01:04,940
Switch on lines 222 to 307. This is the familiar tilt code.

12
00:01:05,440 --> 00:01:10,240
Lines 225 to 253 read and write mailboxes.

13
00:01:10,740 --> 00:01:16,180
Lines 255 to 285 find and wake the sensor.

14
00:01:16,680 --> 00:01:23,400
Lines 287 to 307 return three readings, or zeros if the sensor can't be read.

15
00:01:24,400 --> 00:01:28,160
Switch on lines 334 to 358.

16
00:01:28,660 --> 00:01:38,340
Lines 334 to 342 set up the converter. Lines 344 to 358 read channel 23.

17
00:01:39,340 --> 00:01:44,060
Switch on lines 403 to 424.

18
00:01:44,560 --> 00:01:53,360
Lines 407 to 412: 9600 speed, sending on, receiving off, and apply.

19
00:01:53,860 --> 00:02:01,940
Line 417 makes room for text. Line 418 writes four numbers separated by commas.

20
00:02:02,440 --> 00:02:07,800
Lines 420 to 423 send it, only if building the text worked.

21
00:02:08,800 --> 00:02:16,139
Switch on lines 455 to 469. They wake the board, print a banner, prepare the light,

22
00:02:16,139 --> 00:02:18,880
unlock sleep and set the alarm.

23
00:02:19,880 --> 00:02:24,760
Switch on lines 493 to 495: tilt, voltage and Bluetooth, once.

24
00:02:25,760 --> 00:02:30,880
Switch on lines 531 to 546.

25
00:02:31,380 --> 00:02:36,420
Line 536 sleeps. Line 538 blinks.

26
00:02:36,920 --> 00:02:47,400
Lines 540 and 541 read both sensors. Line 543 sends. Line 545 prints a copy for you.

27
00:02:48,400 --> 00:02:53,440
Build and load. Every five seconds a line appears. These numbers are examples.

28
00:02:53,940 --> 00:03:01,187
With your leader, wire the module while powered off. Send connects to receive. Set

29
00:03:01,187 --> 00:03:03,220
it to 9600 and pair it.

30
00:03:03,720 --> 00:03:07,240
Then run dashboard dot py with the Bluetooth port.

31
00:03:08,240 --> 00:03:13,280
On line 120, try twenty seconds. What is the trade off?

32
00:03:13,780 --> 00:03:18,780
Your turn. Pause the video.

33
00:03:18,780 --> 00:03:21,180
Longer battery life, but fewer readings.

34
00:03:21,680 --> 00:03:28,480
You've built a sensor that sleeps, senses and reports, and read it line by line.

35
00:03:28,480 --> 00:03:29,840
Congratulations.

